# Exact finite-sample compression-calibration check v1

Idea ctt-streaming-calibration-audit-v1, revision 1, window goal contract 1; inherited project contract 2. CPU only, one thread through unchanged cpu_guard.py, 4 GiB RLIMIT_AS; planned process RSS under 32 MiB. This stdlib-only 512-state mathematical check is appropriate despite host headroom near 0.53 GB. It is NOT a KT-Compress reproduction or native empirical benchmark.

Question: is preservation of each bin mean in expectation sufficient to preserve the randomization-test level when compression budget depends on the sample label?

Definition: four raw bins of two iid Rademacher observations each. Group A contains bin 0, group B bins 1..3. For each bin let z be either one uniformly selected observation (budget 1) or the exact mean of both (budget 2). Primary rule uses budget 1 only for bin 0, budget 2 otherwise. Each summary is unbiased for its own empirical mean conditional on its data. Test T_i = (z_i - sum_{j!=i} z_j/3)^2. Enumerate the four singleton-versus-three bin assignments. Reject with probability 1/t at alpha=1/4 if observed T_0 ties for largest among t entries, and zero otherwise. This is exact randomized upper-rank testing; no Monte Carlo permutations or asymptotics.

Controls: all bins budget 1 (use a shared independent fair index coin; joint bin summaries remain iid conditional on that coin); all bins budget 2. A shared coin also suffices for the primary bin-0 choice. Enumerate 2^8 raw assignments times 2 coin outcomes, all probability 1/512; retain every case and exact Fraction rejection probabilities.

Exposure: analytical derivation was considered before this plan; this is prospective executable verification of that proposed counterexample, not an unseen discovery/confirmation benchmark. Prediction: both symmetric controls have exact rejection 1/4; asymmetric compression may exceed 1/4, with hand-derived candidate 25/64. If mismatch, repair or reject the hand calculation, never change the expected number to match code. Independent closed-form calculation conditions on z0=1 and uses the multinomial counts of the other bin means (-1,0,1).

Decision: a valid excess refutes only the blanket claim that unbiased adaptive compression preserves bin-permutation exactness. It does not refute CTT, whose common compression policy is not used here. Derive a sufficient permutation-equivariance condition and audit native CTT/Cheap Permutation Testing before proposing performance improvements. No novelty, native speed or real-data claim. Future required coverage: immutable author code and actual kernel/bandwidth/seed handling; strong cheap and Nyström baselines; original Gaussian/EMNIST protocol; complete cost and power-vs-size diagnostics. No grid or empirical run is approved by this theoretical result alone.
